Derive trigonometric addition from composed rotations (@math-rotation-identities: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Identify-phase-period-and-the-domain-of-an-inverse-question.lingua#r_00136A9ACFD17B64955E9DAD26") { r_00CF00FCDE36C6BE809208BACF
A rotation through α takes the first basis vector to (cos α,sin α). The positively oriented perpendicular unit vector is (−sin α,cos α), the image of the second basis vector. A vector (x,y) therefore maps to

R(α)(x,y)=(x cos α−y sin α, x sin α+y cos α).

This is a linear map because it acts on the basis coefficients. Expanding the squared length of the image and using cos²α+sin²α=1 gives x²+y²: the mixed terms cancel. Its determinant is one, its transpose is R(−α), and their product is the identity. Reuse the earlier matrix/coordinate foundations; a rotation changes components without changing the geometric length.

Geometric composition of oriented rotations adds their angles. Apply R(α) to the unit point (cos β,sin β). Since the result is the unit point for α+β, comparison of coordinates proves

cos(α+β)=cos α cos β−sin α sin β,
sin(α+β)=sin α cos β+cos α sin β.

Replacing β by −β proves the difference identities. This argument uses rotation composition as a geometric fact; it does not assume trigonometric derivatives or a differential equation. Compare it with the equal-chord/distance derivation in [Algebra and Trigonometry 2e — Abramson and contributors](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/sources/Algebra-and-Trigonometry.lingua#r_7FKH7S2VNZT97STHJ10KB186YX), section 9.2.

Setting β=α gives sin 2α=2sin α cos α and cos 2α=cos²α−sin²α. Substituting the circle identity yields cos 2α=1−2sin²α=2cos²α−1. For a positive first-quadrant half-angle, sin(α/2)=√((1−cos α)/2); outside that domain the sign needs separate information. Squaring an equation can conceal this choice.

A rational unit point supplies an exact rotation without needing a named angle. The pair (3/5,4/5) has squared length one. The corresponding rotation takes (2,−1) to (2,1), preserving squared length 5. Its square has cosine −7/25 and sine 24/25, obtained by matrix multiplication or the double-angle identities. The angle exists by the unit-circle definition, but a rational coordinate does not make its angle a rational multiple of π.

**Practice:** derive R(α)R(β)=R(α+β) entry by entry. Use a 30° and 45° composition to obtain exact 75° values, retaining radical signs. Explain why reversing the order matters for some three-dimensional rotations even though these planar rotations commute; a three-dimensional proof is later work, not supplied by the planar calculation.

